WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.210 --> 00:00:06.410
Hey everybody. My name is Allison Powell
and I'm an actor and filmmaker. And I am

00:00:06.410 --> 00:00:10.400
going to walk you through the five steps to
get your project from script-to-screen.

00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:14.900
and I'm mainly going to be talking about
shorter projects (short films or

00:00:14.900 --> 00:00:18.380
webseries). There's a lot of information
about how to get independent features

00:00:18.380 --> 00:00:21.830
made and while it's essentially the same
steps there are a lot more in depth and

00:00:21.830 --> 00:00:25.789
you have a lot more variables that
you're dealing with but there's not a

00:00:25.789 --> 00:00:30.710
ton of information about how to make a
short project or a small budget project

00:00:30.710 --> 00:00:34.460
and there's a lot of people that'll just
say go make your movie like get friends

00:00:34.460 --> 00:00:38.329
together go shoot it on your iPhone
which is great and totally doable but

00:00:38.329 --> 00:00:42.800
sometimes it's like I don't know what to
do what are the steps how do I make it

00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:45.980
happen and that's certainly where I was
coming from when I started making

00:00:45.980 --> 00:00:50.960
shorter projects and so I am gonna
explain to you everything that you need

00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:54.649
to know first step when you're making a
short project is to determine what your

00:00:54.649 --> 00:00:57.950
budget is I always tell people your
budget is the money that you have

00:00:57.950 --> 00:01:01.340
whether that's the money that you have
in your savings account the money that

00:01:01.340 --> 00:01:05.659
you can put aside from your check every
week or the money that you crowd fund

00:01:05.659 --> 00:01:10.100
and raise with the help of friends it's
always gonna be the money that you have

00:01:10.100 --> 00:01:14.659
anything is possible on any budget some
things are just easier with more money

00:01:14.659 --> 00:01:21.380
or easier to achieve with less money
your budget is determined by what you

00:01:21.380 --> 00:01:25.430
want to do with the project at the end
so think about that if you just want to

00:01:25.430 --> 00:01:30.560
make a project to learn how to get a
project together what it means to be on

00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:35.180
set test out producing something test
out if you like directing see if you

00:01:35.180 --> 00:01:39.439
want to get some her footage for your
real or dramatic footage for your real I

00:01:39.439 --> 00:01:44.420
highly recommend spending less money you
are going to learn the same lessons if

00:01:44.420 --> 00:01:47.780
you spend two hundred and fifty dollars
or if you spend twenty five hundred

00:01:47.780 --> 00:01:52.520
dollars and it's way better to learn the
lessons on the two hundred and fifty

00:01:52.520 --> 00:01:57.530
dollar project than on the twenty-five
hundred dollar project so think about

00:01:57.530 --> 00:02:01.520
what you want to do with your end goal
if you want to submit it to festivals if

00:02:01.520 --> 00:02:05.479
you want it to be sort of a proof of
concept for a feature or pitching a

00:02:05.479 --> 00:02:09.379
pilot then you probably want to spend a
little bit more money you're just gonna

00:02:09.379 --> 00:02:13.040
get a slightly higher quality project
you're gonna be able to rent better

00:02:13.040 --> 00:02:15.340
equipment
and locations and make it look really

00:02:15.340 --> 00:02:18.879
really nice if you just want to make a
project to learn how to do it

00:02:18.879 --> 00:02:25.269
you can spend 60 bucks I made five
projects for $60 to a hundred and fifty

00:02:25.269 --> 00:02:29.530
dollars and I learned almost everything
I need to know about producing doing

00:02:29.530 --> 00:02:34.450
those projects so it's totally possible
when you're thinking about your project

00:02:34.450 --> 00:02:37.689
think about the end goal and that's
going to determine what your budget is

00:02:37.689 --> 00:02:42.670
and again your budget is the money that
you have anything's possible for any

00:02:42.670 --> 00:02:46.720
amount it just might take more work your
budget when you're putting it together I

00:02:46.720 --> 00:02:50.620
put all my budgets together in Google
sheets just as a spreadsheet so

00:02:50.620 --> 00:02:55.720
basically an Excel document that way you
can also put in all of the formulas so I

00:02:55.720 --> 00:03:00.099
can say it's gonna be a two-day shoot
I'm paying my director two hundred

00:03:00.099 --> 00:03:04.689
dollars a day so two hundred two days
and then I can put in a formula here and

00:03:04.689 --> 00:03:11.349
to multiply those days or add the column
so then I have a running charlie going

00:03:11.349 --> 00:03:15.900
as well and it should look something
kind of like this

00:03:19.080 --> 00:03:22.890
if you're only spending money for food
you might not even need to put together

00:03:22.890 --> 00:03:27.180
a budget but it's always a good practice
to try and do it one of the first bigger

00:03:27.180 --> 00:03:30.840
projects that I made I never made a
formal budget and now when people ask me

00:03:30.840 --> 00:03:34.500
how much money I spent on it I don't
have an answer and it would have been

00:03:34.500 --> 00:03:37.860
great to know and a great way to track
what I was spending and what I was

00:03:37.860 --> 00:03:41.490
spending it on so even if it's a tiny
project go ahead and put together a

00:03:41.490 --> 00:03:44.490
little spreadsheet again
you might as well learn on a smaller

00:03:44.490 --> 00:03:47.520
project where you're spending less money
than on something where you're spending

00:03:47.520 --> 00:03:51.960
between eight and ten thousand dollars
the second thing to do for your project

00:03:51.960 --> 00:03:57.810
is to hire crew you only have to hire
crew people one person at a time when I

00:03:57.810 --> 00:04:01.080
was first starting out I feel like I got
overwhelmed because it's like you need a

00:04:01.080 --> 00:04:04.620
sound person and a camera person and the
lighting person and a makeup artist and

00:04:04.620 --> 00:04:07.650
do you need somebody to do costumes and
who's gonna handle props and what's

00:04:07.650 --> 00:04:11.610
production design and it's so
overwhelming when I did pretty much all

00:04:11.610 --> 00:04:17.130
of my first projects I hired just one
person at a time that's all it takes and

00:04:17.130 --> 00:04:21.019
usually if you get that one person on
board and they're really excited and

00:04:21.019 --> 00:04:25.050
excited to be involved in the project
they're gonna start making suggestions

00:04:25.050 --> 00:04:29.490
of other crew people that they know that
they can bring on board so you really

00:04:29.490 --> 00:04:34.680
only need to know one person or reach
out to one person when I made a black

00:04:34.680 --> 00:04:39.210
widow fan film I reached out to someone
initially to be the first assistant

00:04:39.210 --> 00:04:42.840
director cuz I knew he was a big comic
book fan and I knew he'd be excited

00:04:42.840 --> 00:04:46.890
about the project and I said here's my
idea here's the script you want to meet

00:04:46.890 --> 00:04:50.100
up and talk about it and he's the one
who actually said hey I really want to

00:04:50.100 --> 00:04:53.669
direct something and if I get to direct
it then my friend who's a director of

00:04:53.669 --> 00:04:57.480
photography wants to work with me so
I'll bring him on and he's got

00:04:57.480 --> 00:05:00.270
connections to lights and he has
connections to camera and he's gonna

00:05:00.270 --> 00:05:05.760
hire everybody in the genie department
so again one person it's very very

00:05:05.760 --> 00:05:10.890
simple you also want to hire kind of
from the top down so you want to hire

00:05:10.890 --> 00:05:15.390
your director first if that's not going
to be you you are the producer no matter

00:05:15.390 --> 00:05:20.250
what so you're hiring everybody you may
want to hire another producer if you

00:05:20.250 --> 00:05:23.610
want a little more help and assistance
and going through this step-by-step but

00:05:23.610 --> 00:05:28.169
you certainly don't have to but hire
your director your director may have an

00:05:28.169 --> 00:05:31.530
opinion or then want to choose their
director or photography the director of

00:05:31.530 --> 00:05:33.710
photography
I have a gaffer that they want to work

00:05:33.710 --> 00:05:39.080
with or a first AC so it's better to
hire kind of from the top down so that

00:05:39.080 --> 00:05:42.619
then you're not saying to a director
well I've already hired this DP so you

00:05:42.619 --> 00:05:46.339
have to work with them or I've already
hired this gaffer so you're gonna just

00:05:46.339 --> 00:05:51.020
have to make it work ideally that way
everybody knows who everybody is how I

00:05:51.020 --> 00:05:54.949
work well together and all of those
things you want your crew and your set

00:05:54.949 --> 00:05:59.389
to be a really cohesive structure and I
really like my sets to be a fun place to

00:05:59.389 --> 00:06:03.369
come and work because especially when
you're not spending that much money

00:06:03.369 --> 00:06:07.189
you're maybe not paying people anything
or you're paying them a very reduced

00:06:07.189 --> 00:06:11.689
rate it's a huge help to at least make
it somewhere fun to hang out for the day

00:06:11.689 --> 00:06:16.219
since you've already put together your
budget what you're able to spend and

00:06:16.219 --> 00:06:19.129
what you're willing to spend you'll know
what you'll be able to pay crew people

00:06:19.129 --> 00:06:23.959
when you're hiring them and it is very
key to be just upfront and honest with

00:06:23.959 --> 00:06:28.939
what the rate is if it's free if it's
nothing if it's just gas money if it's

00:06:28.939 --> 00:06:34.189
50 bucks when you're hiring someone just
always be honest there's no point in

00:06:34.189 --> 00:06:38.719
going around and saying do you want to
work for me like yeah it's paying but

00:06:38.719 --> 00:06:43.819
it's really only paying $50 just say
it's paying it's a very low rate it's X

00:06:43.819 --> 00:06:48.769
amount that way everybody knows what
they're getting upfront and if somebody

00:06:48.769 --> 00:06:53.659
can't work for that rate ideally they'd
tell you right away and you don't get

00:06:53.659 --> 00:06:57.319
offended you don't get upset you don't
tell them why it's a terrible mistake

00:06:57.319 --> 00:07:01.069
and your movies gonna be amazing and
then we're gonna regret not working for

00:07:01.069 --> 00:07:04.249
you you just say thank you so much
hopefully we find something to work on

00:07:04.249 --> 00:07:08.240
together in the future
and let them go you can always find

00:07:08.240 --> 00:07:12.139
somebody that is able to work with your
budget it might not be the first person

00:07:12.139 --> 00:07:15.589
that you ask but since you have that
budget together and you know what you're

00:07:15.589 --> 00:07:20.240
able to spend you can say that upfront
when you're hiring crew people if you

00:07:20.240 --> 00:07:24.589
have any sort of flexibility in your
budget or the director of photography is

00:07:24.589 --> 00:07:28.279
saying oh I need X amount and you really
want to work with them then that's kind

00:07:28.279 --> 00:07:32.419
of up to you and that's on a project a
project basis but be upfront about the

00:07:32.419 --> 00:07:35.180
rate they are offering people the third
thing that you're gonna need to do is

00:07:35.180 --> 00:07:41.209
find a place to film this is hugely
determined by you and the script and the

00:07:41.209 --> 00:07:44.759
reason that I say by you is because
you as a filmmaker have personal

00:07:44.759 --> 00:07:47.909
connections that I might not know about
your director might not know about

00:07:47.909 --> 00:07:52.469
another producer might not know about
and if you have those connections and

00:07:52.469 --> 00:07:56.460
you're able to use them to get a place
to film perfect you might be able to

00:07:56.460 --> 00:08:02.580
film in a mansion for free or a bar for
free or in a swimming pool for free it

00:08:02.580 --> 00:08:07.020
all depends on who you know and where
they live and what they have access to

00:08:07.020 --> 00:08:12.810
so if you know that you have a friend
who has a backyard with a trampoline in

00:08:12.810 --> 00:08:17.099
it and that is the specific thing that
you need for your script then you have

00:08:17.099 --> 00:08:20.339
to reach out to that friend and see if
they're willing to let you go and film

00:08:20.339 --> 00:08:24.900
there and it could be a totally
reasonable location if you don't have a

00:08:24.900 --> 00:08:29.249
ton of connections and you're wanting to
make something just for practice or for

00:08:29.249 --> 00:08:33.510
a lower budget then ideally you write it
somewhere that you do have access to

00:08:33.510 --> 00:08:37.949
that you know about so maybe that's just
in your apartment in your house you know

00:08:37.949 --> 00:08:41.310
get creative maybe it's not the dining
room table maybe it's the bathroom maybe

00:08:41.310 --> 00:08:44.550
it's the kitchen
maybe it's the backyard maybe it's up on

00:08:44.550 --> 00:08:48.449
the roof or on the porch on the balcony
but you're going to need to find

00:08:48.449 --> 00:08:53.010
somewhere to film it the other thing to
keep in mind is that a lot of people who

00:08:53.010 --> 00:08:58.350
don't work in the film industry don't
know that our days are 12 to 12 and a

00:08:58.350 --> 00:09:03.269
half hours long because you should never
ever go over 12 and a half hours if you

00:09:03.269 --> 00:09:07.560
can it all help it do not go over time
if you remember nothing else remember

00:09:07.560 --> 00:09:13.980
that so if you're reaching out to your
mom's old college roommate who has a

00:09:13.980 --> 00:09:18.180
trampoline in her backyard that has a
giant sprinkler and a fountain and

00:09:18.180 --> 00:09:22.380
that's everything that you need for your
script make sure that she knows you

00:09:22.380 --> 00:09:27.510
might need to be there from 7 a.m. to
7:30 p.m. and make sure that they are ok

00:09:27.510 --> 00:09:32.310
with that if you don't have a friend who
has a castle or a swimming pool or owns

00:09:32.310 --> 00:09:36.089
a mini golf course or whatever you need
you can look at places like Pierce base

00:09:36.089 --> 00:09:41.790
and Rat pal they're really good location
resources and also just Yelp calling up

00:09:41.790 --> 00:09:46.440
local restaurants local cafes local
shops is a great way to get a location

00:09:46.440 --> 00:09:50.579
for filming anywhere that's
independently owned is going to be a lot

00:09:50.579 --> 00:09:54.149
easier to deal with then somewhere
that's a chain the fourth thing that

00:09:54.149 --> 00:09:57.900
you're going to need to do
is rent equipment sometimes when you're

00:09:57.900 --> 00:10:02.730
hiring crew you might ask the GP or the
sound person or the production designer

00:10:02.730 --> 00:10:07.140
whoever what gear they already have and
what are they willing to bring sometimes

00:10:07.140 --> 00:10:12.030
a DP is willing to bring their camera
onto the project and reduce the rental

00:10:12.030 --> 00:10:15.960
fee or maybe even bring it on for free
if they really like the project but

00:10:15.960 --> 00:10:19.260
you're gonna want to ask them that you
know do you have a camera do you want to

00:10:19.260 --> 00:10:22.710
film this project on your camera
do you have lenses are you willing to

00:10:22.710 --> 00:10:27.590
use those lenses on the project if
they're not totally fine they might say

00:10:27.590 --> 00:10:33.060
you know I do have a camera but this is
gonna look way better on red and I only

00:10:33.060 --> 00:10:37.470
have a black magic or it's gonna look
better on this camera or that camera

00:10:37.470 --> 00:10:41.130
there's a whole bunch about equipment
that they might throw at you and the

00:10:41.130 --> 00:10:44.700
thing to know is you don't need to know
all the answers and everything about

00:10:44.700 --> 00:10:48.510
equipment you just need to get a list
from them of what they need rented and

00:10:48.510 --> 00:10:51.900
again this is a place where you should
be really upfront with your budget with

00:10:51.900 --> 00:10:58.410
them so I have $600 for camera and
lenses what do you need and what can we

00:10:58.410 --> 00:11:03.060
get for about that budget once they send
you over a list you can look at places

00:11:03.060 --> 00:11:08.070
like share grid and kit split because
they are more affordable typically than

00:11:08.070 --> 00:11:11.640
rental houses for getting equipment and
you can just copy and paste the list

00:11:11.640 --> 00:11:17.220
into the search bar one item at a time
if you're not finding what you need or

00:11:17.220 --> 00:11:21.870
it's turning into piecing a bunch of
equipment from you know 20 places all

00:11:21.870 --> 00:11:26.760
over town you can also Ford that list
over to a rental house typically rental

00:11:26.760 --> 00:11:31.920
houses are camera gear like camera
lenses tripods items like that and then

00:11:31.920 --> 00:11:37.740
a rental house for lights so c-stands
lights sandbags things of that nature so

00:11:37.740 --> 00:11:41.430
you may end up renting from two
different rental houses totally normal

00:11:41.430 --> 00:11:45.150
totally fine if you find a place where
you can rent everything at once do that

00:11:45.150 --> 00:11:50.370
that's amazing when I reach out to a
rental house I usually just Ford or a

00:11:50.370 --> 00:11:54.750
copy and paste the equipment list over
to them from the email that I've gotten

00:11:54.750 --> 00:11:59.400
from my crew the rental house will email
me back and say here's the quote here's

00:11:59.400 --> 00:12:01.980
you know all the equipment that we have
and here's what

00:12:01.980 --> 00:12:08.190
cost you it's very rarely the amount
that I am able or willing to spend

00:12:08.190 --> 00:12:11.670
so sometimes it's totally out of the
ballpark and it's just not possible

00:12:11.670 --> 00:12:15.180
sometimes it's pretty close and in that
point you can absolutely email the

00:12:15.180 --> 00:12:19.770
rental house back and say this is great
I actually only have $600 to rent this

00:12:19.770 --> 00:12:25.500
equipment are you able to go to 600
maybe they are maybe they aren't maybe

00:12:25.500 --> 00:12:29.280
we can go to 650 and you think you can
pull $50 from somewhere else in the

00:12:29.280 --> 00:12:33.750
budget great if it's something that's
totally out of the ballpark and that's

00:12:33.750 --> 00:12:37.140
been out of the ballpark on share grade
kit split with every rental house that

00:12:37.140 --> 00:12:39.690
you reach out you at that point you
might have to reach back out to your

00:12:39.690 --> 00:12:42.750
crew and just say I don't think that
we're gonna be able to afford all of

00:12:42.750 --> 00:12:45.690
this equipment
what can you live without what do we

00:12:45.690 --> 00:12:49.260
absolutely need rental houses are great
and they usually will work with you

00:12:49.260 --> 00:12:53.490
again as long as you are upfront and
honest the last thing that you need to

00:12:53.490 --> 00:12:58.950
do and it's not always the last all of
these steps are intermix is picking a

00:12:58.950 --> 00:13:04.290
date to film for me there's two methods
to picking a date to film one is just

00:13:04.290 --> 00:13:09.750
sort of picking an arbitrary date that
works for you and maybe the location but

00:13:09.750 --> 00:13:13.410
definitely one that works for you and
saying this is the day I'm gonna be

00:13:13.410 --> 00:13:17.880
ready to film and I'm giving myself a
deadline and we are filming June 4th

00:13:17.880 --> 00:13:21.750
then when you're hiring crew you're
hiring the location you're renting

00:13:21.750 --> 00:13:26.010
equipment everything you want June 4th
then when you're talking to crew members

00:13:26.010 --> 00:13:29.700
if they're not available June 4th again
you can say thank you so much maybe

00:13:29.700 --> 00:13:34.320
we'll find another project to work on
we're shooting June 4th the other way

00:13:34.320 --> 00:13:38.550
you can pick a day to film is by your
crew so maybe there's some crew members

00:13:38.550 --> 00:13:43.860
that you really want to work with or a
director or DP in that scenario you say

00:13:43.860 --> 00:13:47.850
to everybody okay we're all on board we
all want to make this project what days

00:13:47.850 --> 00:13:51.930
are everyone available and ideally they
give you a couple of different options

00:13:51.930 --> 00:13:56.520
because maybe almost everyone is
available June 4th but a couple people

00:13:56.520 --> 00:14:01.620
aren't but everyone is available on June
6th so then you can go film on June 6 of

00:14:01.620 --> 00:14:04.320
course you want to be confirming with
your location that they're available

00:14:04.320 --> 00:14:09.120
that day again for twelve to twelve and
a half hours on that day if your friends

00:14:09.120 --> 00:14:14.470
like yeah you can film here on June 6
but we have a party at 3 p.m.

00:14:14.470 --> 00:14:17.980
you can't actually film there on June 6
then maybe you need to see if people can

00:14:17.980 --> 00:14:22.930
film June 7th as I was saying all of
these stages overlap they intermingle

00:14:22.930 --> 00:14:30.010
you don't necessarily have to do them in
order mostly in order is helpful you

00:14:30.010 --> 00:14:33.250
know starting with your budget
finding your crew finding your locations

00:14:33.250 --> 00:14:38.440
your equipment picking a date of film
really doesn't have to come last be

00:14:38.440 --> 00:14:43.390
somewhere in the mix but all of these
steps you want to do before you get on

00:14:43.390 --> 00:14:48.610
set and start filming even if you're
making a project for $60.00 that money

00:14:48.610 --> 00:14:53.650
should go towards food you always want
to have water coffee and really good

00:14:53.650 --> 00:14:57.940
food on set that's just a great way to
show your appreciation your taking care

00:14:57.940 --> 00:15:04.090
of your crew everybody's hydrated eating
they're happy they've got caffeine also

00:15:04.090 --> 00:15:07.180
if you're not spending a ton of money
it's a great idea to reach out to your

00:15:07.180 --> 00:15:11.020
